From 5fb6d16de687e6652084e6f541f6f6cc589595cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:15:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] Keep the overnight no-training probes of the tail habit:
 the early-bias probe and the early-warning curve, with their verifiers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds docs/investigations/stance_2026_10/overnight_probes/ (162 files,
about 1.3 MB) with a README, the evidence of decision D-D56 (1) and
(2) (a) and of the tail-habit note's §9.2 and §9.3: copies of the
session-scratch folders scratchpad2/com_revisit/notes/<name>/, whose
top-level files were written from 00:30 to 04:37 UTC on 2026-10-30,
each a no-training probe on the first r10 stance round's local
checkpoints (and, for the held-out test, the r9 stance5 runs'
records), with its adversarial verifier:

- early_bias/ and verify_early_bias/: one PPO update rebuilt at the r10
  checkpoints with no optimizer step, each loss term's push on the
  tail_3 command split by reward term, state region and noise carrier;
  only the policy-gradient term moves the tail mean; at 1,310,720 steps
  s42 already has an upward push carried by tail_3's own exploration
  noise (+0.00115 +- 0.00032 per epoch, t 3.6) and s80 none; the
  critic's bootstrapped value is its source from 2,097,152 on,
  unresolved at the onset.
- early_warning/ and verify_early_warning/: the tail loop's gain,
  offset, fixed point and posture slope at every saved checkpoint of
  r10 s42, s60 and s80 and of the 24 entropy-screen continuations; the
  gain turns positive one checkpoint before the first tail over-bar
  episode in s42 and s60, but late s80 reaches the same loop state with
  none; the record's early gains for s42 came from a sweep that reaches
  the normaliser's clip (unclipped -0.725 and -0.636 at 1,048,576 and
  2,097,152).
- warning_r9/, warning_margin/ and verify_warning_followups/: the
  pre-registered r9 held-out test of the warning signals (the policy
  probe's tail_3 offset fired in all five r9 runs by 2.06M: the test
  fails) and the pre-registered margin to the rail at seven checkpoints
  (late s80 as close as s42 one checkpoint before its first over-bar
  episode, approaching it 4-12x more slowly).

Kept from each notes folder, as for com_revisit/ and tail_probes/: its
top-level text files (notes, tables, scripts, logs and non-empty error
logs), run markers, JSON outputs of at most 130 KB and JSONL outputs of
at most 60 KB. Not copied: every subdirectory (in early_bias/ alone 643
per-rollout files, about 248 MB; the fetched checkpoint pairs; the
stored states), warning_r9/wr9_procx.npz, the 31 empty error logs and
the larger JSON and JSONL outputs (their .txt renderings are kept, or
they are per checkpoint or per episode). No new kind of change: every
copy is byte-identical to its source, absolute session-scratch paths
included, but warning_r9/wr9_apply.py, copied as wr9_apply.py.gz (gzip):
it uses a nested f-string with the same quotes, which Python 3.11 cannot
parse, and test_policy_loading.py parses every Python file under docs/
on each Python version CI runs. None held a session tool id. Every
source's sha256 equals its entry in the SHA256SUMS of the first screen's
durable copy (claude/handoff-bias-screen at cfed2c70, read in its local
clone), which holds every top-level file of the seven folders but the
npz file and early_warning/ew_events.json. 11 JSON files without a final
newline, 2 files ending in a blank line and 8 with lines ending in
spaces are kept as their sources have them; the README names them. The
other Python scripts parse on Python 3.11, 3.12 and 3.13, and none reads
a name test_policy_loading.py guards.

SOURCES.txt gains the note and 162 rows; SHA256SUMS now pins 1528
files (sha256sum -c: all OK); the folder's README gains the entry, the
folder's note under "What changed in the copies", the README among the
files SHA256SUMS does not pin, and three re-check rows; this change's
status row names the folder.

Records only: no library code, configuration, test, notebook, digest or
generated file changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
